# Make chargen.keys' orc barbarian and put a Circlet of Blasting on his head.
# Included by yuse-activate-blast.keys and a-activate-blast.keys, which differ
# only in which command they then use to activate it. bd inc-azc.
#
# The character owns nothing that blasts, so the item is made in wizard mode:
#
#   w y                      enter wizard mode and open its menu
#   Item Acquisition         the acquisition browser
#   RIGHT*19                 walk the glyph row to Headbands. ItemGlyphs
#                            (src/Tables.cpp:340) puts -AI_HEADBAND 19 steps
#                            along, and the header line names the category, so
#                            a miscount shows up in a dump as some other word
#                            after "Acquisition -- [...]:".
#   @until "Circlet of       the candidate list has no menu letters and no '>'
#     Blasting" DOWN         marker, so neither @choose nor @cursorto can reach
#                            a row -- walking the cursor with DOWN is the only
#                            way in. The window shows three rows and highlights
#                            the middle one, so @until stops on the pass where
#                            the wanted name first appears at the BOTTOM of
#                            that window, and one further DOWN moves the
#                            highlight onto it. Naming the item itself, rather
#                            than a neighbour used as a sentinel, is what makes
#                            this independent of the row's position: the
#                            acquisition order follows lib/m_items.irh, and a
#                            new headband moves every row after it.
#   @expect "Circlet of      nail the name to the row before taking it, so a
#     Blasting"              drifted cursor stops the run here instead of
#                            quietly acquiring the neighbour. The check does not
#                            rely on this alone: tools/check_yuse_activate.sh
#                            re-reads the item's name off the activate menu.
#   ENTER                    take it. A non-stacking item lands in the In-the-
#                            Air slot and opens the inventory manager.
#   q SPACE ESC              put it on the head and leave. SPACE is the swap
#                            (src/Managers.cpp:911) and ESC leaves once the air
#                            slot is empty (src/Managers.cpp:761). The close
#                            used to be "q SPACE ENTER", and 59c34fa made the
#                            manager's Enter an unconditional swap, so the old
#                            ENTER put the circlet on and then lifted it
#                            straight back into the air, leaving the manager
#                            open for the next keystroke to land in. Worn, not
#                            stowed, because the 'a' route measured below lists
#                            only what the player can actively use.

@include chargen.keys

w
y
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*19
@until "Circlet of Blasting" DOWN
@expect "Circlet of Blasting"
DOWN
ENTER
q SPACE ESC
